A1. If you are unemployed remember a poet once lived on this for several years. ~ AVON
A5. Some say this stands for soused over Sunday. ~ SOS
A7. Where old nags love to loiter. ~ POST
A11. A fine place to build your castle in Spain. ~ GRANADA
A13. If you run a drug store this is probably part of your overhead. ~ PLASTER
A16. If you enjoy a joke this'll start your risibilities. ~ RIS
A17. Our reaction to reading the Wickersham report. ~ NAP
A18. This picks up the darndest noises. ~ EAR
A19. Just a plain, ordinary, crude copper. ~ ORE
A20. That four-legged human. ~ ASS
A21. The first thing Mr. Hoover will do when asked to run again. ~ YIELD
A23. It seems impossible but Brooklyn is really a part of this (abbr.). ~ USA
A24. Vegetable produced by string factories (pl.). ~ BEANS
A27. Here's a twister for you. ~ EEL
A28. If you're a widower you might find a second wife this way. ~ CUTER
A30. Arrive home in this condition and your wife may think you're loaded. ~ LADEN
A32. The higher and fewer. ~ ELITE
A34. A word every Wellesley graduate likes to use. ~ LAST
A36. This tenant is usually left in England when trouble starts. (abbr.) ~ LT
A37. Lily Pons uses this when she practises. ~ DO
A38. Her rosebud of a mouth ten years after marriage. ~ TRAP
